<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#jury-trial">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jury trial</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as trial by jury</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Jury trial is a legal procedure in which a panel of citizens decides the facts of a case. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-ii-of-russia">Alexander II</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-judicial-reform-of-1864">Judicial Reform of 1864</a> introduced trial by jury along with independent courts and adversarial procedure. It was one of the most significant legal reforms of the imperial period.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jury-trial.jpg" alt="Jury trial" width="320" height="181" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: John Morgan · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Jury_by_John_Morgan.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>A jury trial is set apart from a bench trial, in which a judge or panel of judges makes all decisions. In ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> the courts were made up of dikastai chosen by lot, up to 500 citizens in ordinary cases and 1,001 to 1,501 in capital cases, ruling by majority. Jurors voted in secret with ceramic disks whose axles were hollow or solid, held so as to hide which, and from Periclean times they were paid a day's wages for sitting.</p><p>In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-republic">Roman Republic</a> and Empire judges were lay citizens rather than professionals. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-law">Roman law</a> provided for the yearly selection of judices to resolve disputes, with a praetor performing many of the duties of a judge, and barred high officials, their relatives and those found guilty of serious crimes.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>In the German lands, Hauenstein's charter of 1442 secured the right to be tried by 24 fellow equals, a Swabian ordinance of 1562 called for jurymen, and in Freiburg juries of 30 citizens and councillors sat. The modern jury trial came to the Rhenish provinces in 1798, usually with 12 citizens. In classical Islamic law, notaries certified the consistent testimony of three to twelve witnesses, a practice John Makdisi has compared with the jury trials of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> under King Henry II.</p><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/common-law">common law</a> systems the jury finds the facts, the judge determines the law and usually sets the penalty. After the judicial reform of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-ii-of-russia">Alexander II</a>, jurors in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> had a third verdict besides guilty and not guilty: guilty, but not to be punished, since Alexander II believed that justice without morality was wrong. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> the jury sits with professional judges to decide guilt and then the penalty.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>In 1665 a petit jury of twelve English and Portuguese jurors at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chennai">Madras</a> acquitted Ascentia Dawes of murdering her enslaved servant, and under Company rule in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> the Crown Courts of the presidency towns used juries while the Company Courts outside them did not. In the Kuba Kingdom, in what is now the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/democratic-republic-of-the-congo">Democratic Republic of the Congo</a>, trial by jury arose independently before Europeans arrived in 1884. Other states gave juries up: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> used them from 1897 to 1919, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/singapore">Singapore</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a> abolished them in 1969 and Malaysia in 1995, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> brought citizens into serious criminal trials as lay judges in 2009.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Jury trial” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The common law, <b>trial by jury</b>, parliamentary government and the principle that rulers are subject to law, symbolised by Magna Carta, were carried to Wales, Ireland and the English colonies and later shaped the legal and political systems of the United States, Canada, Australia, India and many other…</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-england">Kingdom of England · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The most significant legal reform of the period was Alexander II's Judicial Reform of 1864, which introduced independent courts, <b>trial by jury</b>, adversarial procedure and a professionally trained judiciary.</blockquote><a href="/empire/russian-empire">Russian Empire · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Law follows English common law; <b>trial by jury</b> was abolished in 1970, and caning and hanging remain in use.</blockquote><a href="/empire/singapore">Singapore · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>In 2007 David Yount, author of How the Quakers Invented America, argued that Quakers first introduced ideas such as <b>trial by jury</b>, equal rights for men and women, and public education.</blockquote><a href="/movement/quakers">Quakers · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>The Hungarian 12 points of 15 March 1848 added an independent Hungarian ministry, <b>trial by jury</b>, a Hungarian army, civil and religious equality and union with Transylvania.</blockquote><a href="/movement/revolutions-of-1848">Revolutions of 1848 · Aims</a></li><li><blockquote>Drafted mainly by George Mason, the Declaration set out rights such as freedom of the press and <b>trial by jury</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/june-12">12 June in history</a></li></ul></section>
